---
description: Preserve Chrome layout architecture and state ownership
alwaysApply: true
---

# Chrome Layout Architecture

- Keep `ChromeLayout` thin: composition shell + panel slots, no heavy panel prop forwarding.
- Child layout pieces (for example `ChromeEdgePanel`) should read app state via `useAppStore` and shared controller context.
- Prefer geometry-derived panel config from one source of truth (`position`/edge mapping) over duplicated per-panel props.
- Keep resize/drag orchestration in dedicated controller/hook modules, not inline in layout render components.
- Use global layout patch actions for chrome dimensions/collapse state; avoid local duplicated state for these values.

# Implementation Guardrails

- When adding new chrome sections, derive border/collapse/resize behavior from edge position mappings.
- Avoid reintroducing broad prop drilling (`onResize*`, collapse booleans, border classes) through multiple component levels.
- Use component props mainly to configure a reusable component. That means - if adding a prop will allow to reuse it somewhere, where the diff is small, e.g. 3 different wrapper props or an extra icon/text.
- If a piece of tsx repeats 3 or more times it's often worth extracting it to a new component.
- If a piece of ts/tsx logic repeates 3 or more times it's often worth extracting to a separate function or hook.
- If it repeats within a single file the new component should be placed in the same file, below the main export. If it needs exporting it should be placed in a separate file in the same directory. If the directory is generic, create a new directory and move both related files into it.
- Most files should not exceed 200 lines of code. No file should exceed 400 lines of code. If the file is over 150 consider refactoring
- Avoid file, component and exported item names longer than 20 characters. For local variables avoid names longer than 15 chars.
- Don't do any backward compatibility or legacy syncing when persistence model changes. Simply run `npm run reset-config` when compatibility breaks due to changes.